WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.993
Chris: There are many different reasons
why conflicts arise in the workplace

00:00:03.423 --> 00:00:07.423
and failure to deal with these issues
quickly and effectively can have a

00:00:07.423 --> 00:00:12.283
serious impact on the wellbeing of
those involved, the wider team, and

00:00:12.283 --> 00:00:13.993
any projects that they are working on.

00:00:14.783 --> 00:00:18.793
Leah: Conflicts will happen and having
people who are able to identify and

00:00:18.853 --> 00:00:23.203
handle conflicts fairly and sensibly
will remove or minimise the impact

00:00:23.203 --> 00:00:24.753
that conflicts may likely have.

00:00:25.523 --> 00:00:29.883
Conflict situations can be very
different in cause or effect and there

00:00:29.883 --> 00:00:31.843
is no one solution that fixes all.

00:00:32.483 --> 00:00:35.373
This is where conflict management
skills come in to play.

00:00:36.133 --> 00:00:39.993
Those familiar with conflict management
will look at each situation and

00:00:39.993 --> 00:00:44.253
utilise a style that is likely to be
effective, so let’s have a look at them.

00:00:45.363 --> 00:00:49.813
There are five conflict styles that
can be utilised to resolve conflicts

00:00:50.603 --> 00:00:51.443
Chris: Accommodating

00:00:51.993 --> 00:00:55.383
This style is about putting the
needs of others before your own.

00:00:55.933 --> 00:00:58.743
This can be useful if there is
one party that has different

00:00:58.743 --> 00:01:00.823
feelings about an issue to others.

00:01:01.443 --> 00:01:04.413
For those who don’t feel
strongly about something, keeping

00:01:04.413 --> 00:01:06.183
the peace is more important.

00:01:06.983 --> 00:01:11.113
Adopting an accommodating style can
be useful to resolve small issues

00:01:11.113 --> 00:01:15.603
quickly and can create a sense of
openness within the team as people

00:01:15.613 --> 00:01:19.763
feel that voicing their opinion will
not automatically create conflict.

00:01:20.533 --> 00:01:24.333
The downside of this method is
that you can be seen as weak and

00:01:24.333 --> 00:01:26.163
prepared to give in quite easily.

00:01:26.863 --> 00:01:30.713
This is a technique that would not be
appropriate for dealing with more serious

00:01:30.713 --> 00:01:32.443
issues that are affecting the team.

00:01:33.533 --> 00:01:34.343
Leah: Avoiding

00:01:35.693 --> 00:01:39.609
This approach can be useful but can
also cause more problems that it solves.

00:01:40.089 --> 00:01:44.089
It involves removing one or more of the
conflicted parties from the project.

00:01:44.629 --> 00:01:49.639
This can be useful as it gives the removed
party or parties time and space to reflect

00:01:49.639 --> 00:01:52.279
on the issues and it may resolve itself.

00:01:53.139 --> 00:01:58.849
Alternatively, if used in the wrong
situation this may cause more conflict as

00:01:58.849 --> 00:02:03.579
it can cause resentment with team members,
and it may also have a detrimental

00:02:03.609 --> 00:02:05.759
impact on the projects progress.

00:02:06.489 --> 00:02:07.309
Chris: Compromise

00:02:07.359 --> 00:02:10.719
This style is all about getting
those that are in conflict to agree

00:02:10.719 --> 00:02:14.539
to concede some of their issues
so that a solution can be reached.

00:02:15.139 --> 00:02:19.869
This style can be useful if there are
critical time factors, or it is imperative

00:02:19.869 --> 00:02:21.659
that a solution is required immediately.

00:02:22.669 --> 00:02:26.739
The downside is that none of those
involved in the conflict is entirely

00:02:26.739 --> 00:02:31.189
happy as they may feel that they have
conceded to much which may make them

00:02:31.189 --> 00:02:33.359
less likely to compromise in the future.

00:02:34.149 --> 00:02:34.949
Leah: Competing

00:02:35.759 --> 00:02:39.913
This is a strategy that is only effective
if there is a serious issue and can

00:02:39.923 --> 00:02:41.993
only be used by those in authority.

00:02:42.683 --> 00:02:46.203
This involves forcing the view or
opinion onto others without giving

00:02:46.263 --> 00:02:48.113
any opportunity for disagreement.

00:02:48.583 --> 00:02:52.063
This leads to a win-lose
outcome and may leave those

00:02:52.063 --> 00:02:53.103
who disagree feeling aggrieved.

00:02:54.353 --> 00:02:57.743
This is a style that should be used
when there is a conflict situation

00:02:57.783 --> 00:03:02.313
that is unnecessary or having a
seriously damaging effect on the team.

00:03:03.633 --> 00:03:04.443
Chris: Collaboration

00:03:05.683 --> 00:03:08.671
This method can be the most useful
style to use as it brings those that

00:03:08.691 --> 00:03:12.671
have differing opinions together to
discuss the problem with an open mind

00:03:13.171 --> 00:03:17.281
encouraging them to focus on resolving
the conflict by finding solutions

00:03:17.281 --> 00:03:19.271
that are agreeable to all involved.

00:03:20.011 --> 00:03:24.421
Personal emotions are left out of this
as the only purpose is to find out

00:03:24.451 --> 00:03:28.911
what is the best solution for the team
which leads to a ‘win-win’ outcome.

00:03:29.641 --> 00:03:33.641
A downside to this is that it
can be very time consuming, and

00:03:33.641 --> 00:03:37.441
deadlines may be at risk while
time is taken to find a solution.

00:03:38.541 --> 00:03:42.711
Leah: In any conflict situation
and no matter what style is used

00:03:42.811 --> 00:03:46.071
there are several steps to take
that will make a significant

00:03:46.071 --> 00:03:48.281
contribution to reaching a resolution.

00:03:49.131 --> 00:03:53.561
The first step is to remain calm
and be prepared to actively listen.

00:03:54.171 --> 00:03:57.651
When someone else is talking others
involved in the conversation are

00:03:57.931 --> 00:04:00.711
already mentally preparing a response.

00:04:02.001 --> 00:04:06.511
Active listening involves focusing
on what is being said and the

00:04:06.531 --> 00:04:10.261
points being made rather than
thinking about your own response.

00:04:11.091 --> 00:04:15.031
Being attentive and listening to the
other person gives you the opportunity

00:04:15.081 --> 00:04:19.381
to understand their point of view and
creates the opportunity to compromise

00:04:19.441 --> 00:04:21.411
and collaborate to find a solution.

00:04:22.461 --> 00:04:25.451
Chris: Being positive and
patient are two other factors in

00:04:25.451 --> 00:04:27.171
successful conflict resolution.

00:04:27.701 --> 00:04:32.111
By slowing down your emotional response
and considering that others involved

00:04:32.131 --> 00:04:36.901
may have positive intentions rather than
negative ones, will make a significant

00:04:36.911 --> 00:04:39.021
contribution to finding a resolution.

00:04:39.701 --> 00:04:44.461
Finding a solution may take time as
you explore all the options but gives

00:04:44.461 --> 00:04:48.481
confidence to others to give opinions
and discuss their concerns to reach

00:04:48.481 --> 00:04:51.251
agreement on an acceptable way forward.

00:04:52.331 --> 00:04:56.351
Leah: Once a conflict has been resolved
it is important to maintain open

00:04:56.351 --> 00:04:58.301
communications between those involved.

00:04:58.991 --> 00:05:03.071
Having an environment where team members
can talk freely and constructively

00:05:03.111 --> 00:05:08.011
on issues or concerns will reduce
the potential for conflict situations

00:05:08.121 --> 00:05:12.751
and raise morale and therefore
in turn increase productivity.

